WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:02.440
- [Narrator] Bitcoin and Ethereum.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.920
So, you know the top
two players in crypto,

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.060
but what about the Cardano
Network's coin Ada.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.650
Ada is an altcoin, meaning
it's an alternative

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.760
to Bitcoin and is named after

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.200
the 19th century
mathematician, Ada Lovelace,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.040
who is considered the
first computer programmer.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.910
Late this summer, of surge in interest

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.200
in Ada pushed the cryptocurrency
to the third largest,

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.230
behind Bitcoin and ether,
Ethereum's currency.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:30.130
During the rise, Ada reached
a market capitalization

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:35.130
of more than $90 billion and
rose to a high of more than $3.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.630
That's nearly a 2500% increase in price

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.660
on a year over year basis.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.770
- That's primarily driven
by retail investors.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:44.920
And I think a lot of the ones

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.417
that I've talked to are saying,

00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:48.980
"I want to kind of find the next Bitcoin.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.310
So maybe that's Cardona's Ada token."

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.300
- [Narrator] Given the
crypto market's volatility,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.610
the third largest changes often,

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.930
but with nearly 6,000 cryptocurrencies

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.250
in existence this year,
why is Cardona's Ada

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.963
one of the few breaking through?

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.193
We'll explain.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:08.260
Cardano describes itself as a

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.870
third generation blockchain platform.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.030
You can think about the
blockchain generations,

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.730
similar to the evolution
of the cell phone.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.930
Bitcoin, the first generation
is like the flip phone.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.300
It was revolutionary in its creation,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.500
but only does basic things.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.400
The second generation Ethereum 1.0,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.500
that's like your first few smartphones.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.920
It offers apps, but is
kind of slow to load.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.290
The third generation
Cardano and Ethereum 2.0,

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.140
want to be more like phones today,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.590
where speed and usability are crucial.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:40.720
The key difference between

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.800
the first generation and the third

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.130
has to do with how the network works.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.560
Bitcoin runs on something called
a proof of work mechanism,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.610
which runs something like this,

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.860
miners, not those ones,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:57.940
but people who set up computers,
allow the system to work,

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.610
their computers simultaneously race

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:02.830
to guess a complex sequence of numbers

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:05.810
in order to validate a
block of transactions.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.800
The miner whose computer
wins is awarded Bitcoin

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.650
and records the validated transaction

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.070
on the blockchain ledger.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.560
A fancy word that essentially
means the online record book.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.710
But aspect when prices rise,

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.070
so do the barriers to enter for miners.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.670
- [Caitlin McCabe] So we're seeing

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.670
companies fully dedicated to mining.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:27.610
We're seeing miners taking
over warehouses and factories

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.190
to set up mining facilities.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.400
- [Narrator] While secure,
this system requires

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.890
huge amounts of processing power,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.670
simply because it requires all

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.130
mining computers to
work on the same puzzle.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.320
And it rewards those with
more computing power.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.260
Just how much power?

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.060
In a year, the Bitcoin
network uses about the same

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.400
amount of electricity as Washington State.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.650
- We've seen proof of work

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.600
increasingly come under scrutiny

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.800
because of the energy
intensity that it uses.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.700
- [Narrator] This is one
of the biggest challenges

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.500
of the first generation of crypto,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.130
and a main reason why
Cardano is breaking through.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.530
Cardano uses a different
system called proof of stake.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.540
A system also adopted by
Ethereum second model,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.620
Ethereum 2.0, which is expected

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:13.850
to be fully rolled out in the future.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:17.240
- The proof of stake model is, I think,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.100
a very exciting prospect for people

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.780
who are in the space because

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.790
it uses an entirely different system

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.740
in order to verify
transactions on the blockchain.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.590
- [Narrator] In Cardona's
proof of stake model,

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.850
Ada owners use their own holdings

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:32.950
to help validate transactions.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.060
This is called staking or delegating.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.890
Think of a stake as a commitment to keep

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.700
the Cardona network operating and secure.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.400
To earn Ada users join a stake pool,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.230
either one they've made or another user's.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.200
Not every user in the stake pool

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.970
is responsible for verifying transactions.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.760
Instead one user, a pool operator,

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.750
does the work on behalf of the group

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.770
cutting down on computer activity.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:59.620
So in short, in first-generation crypto,

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.400
every computer races to
solve one math puzzle.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.890
In third generation,

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.820
only the state pool
operator does the work.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.550
But the proof of stake system,

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.903
isn't the only factor that
helped Cardona breakthrough.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.670
Cardano recently introduced something

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.970
called smart contracts to its system,

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:19.120
with an upgrade it calls Alonzo.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.920
Smart contracts are seen as game changers

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.230
because they allow the blockchain
to host other operations.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:26.720
Again, think of a smartphone.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.070
It's similar to the idea of an app store,

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.490
except with a blockchain,

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.860
these apps are called dApps
or decentralized apps,

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.250
and there is no company
like Apple or Google

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.670
in between the user and the application.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:42.330
Instead, the smart contract
has programmed code

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.850
to facilitate agreements and transactions

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.090
between the user and the dApp.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.530
Users hope Cardano's
adoption will allow it

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.020
to better compete with
the Ethereum network,

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.320
Where about 80% of dApps are built.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.950
But all that traffic has created issues.

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:01.560
- The reason that Ethereum has
faced some scrutiny this year

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.460
is just because there've been,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.360
there's been so much
activity on the network

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.500
that there's been more congestion

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.260
and higher transaction fees

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:12.220
in order to try to
transact on the network.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.110
So the idea for Cardano is to take

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.300
the Ethereum model and
make it more scalable.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.590
- [Narrator] While Cardano's promises

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.350
may sound great in theory,

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.240
they've yet to undergo the
most important test, time.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.070
- We'll have to wait and see, you know,

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.830
how successful people are
able to use the platform

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:35.210
in order to, you know, build
decentralized applications

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.460
and create non fungible tokens.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.270
- [Narrator] The introduction
of smart contracts

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.840
is stage three in a five part plan,

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.040
according to the platform's roadmap.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.560
Still to come are upgrades on
scalability and governance.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.460
- Investors have to keep in mind,

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.800
this is not going to
be a steady investment.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.970
But I think people who like
to trade in mid-volatility,

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.220
that's one of the draws.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:58.970
- [Narrator] The blockchain
engineering company

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.620
behind Cardona, IOHK,
warned that expectations

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.650
need to be managed with this upgrade.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:08.800
So how smart contracts will
impact Cardona in the long run?

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.303
Time will have to tell.

